# The interface is a build input, not a coat of paint

The owner reviewed the factory's first live app, `apps/2026-09-26-teacher-gradebook`, on 2026-09-30
and said (translated) that the interface was crude, that it did not use modern technologies, and that
**"strings wrap badly, like two words spread over seven lines."** Then: **do not repair that app —
make sure the next apps cannot repeat it.** That app is frozen. This rule is what replaces it.

The measured starting position, re-measured in this tree:

- `scaffold/android-compose/…/ui/PlayKilnApp.kt` **already** imports `androidx.navigation3.ui.NavDisplay`
  and calls `NavDisplay(` at line 51 — and then hands it a hand-rolled `SnapshotStateList` and a
  `listSaver` (lines 21–49) instead of the back stack Navigation 3 owns.
- Every shipped slot copied that. `apps/2026-09-26-teacher-gradebook/…/ui/PlayKilnApp.kt:87` is the
  same `mutableStateListOf` / `listSaver` pair. The cost is not style: Navigation 3 keys its
  transitions, its predictive-back continuity, its scene strategies and its deep-link handling off
  the back stack it is given, so a hand-rolled list makes all four inert at once.
- `WindowSizeClass`, `currentWindowAdaptiveInfo`, `ListDetailSceneStrategy` and the rest of the
  adaptive vocabulary appeared **nowhere in this repository**. A five-destination app with a list and
  a detail screen
  (`Destination.Classes → Destination.Classroom → Destination.Student → Destination.GradeSheet`)
  rendered the detail *instead of* the list on a tablet, in one column stretched to a width no text
  was set for.
- `ClassroomScreen.kt:145` and `Brand.kt:440` clamp a label with `maxLines = 1` and no `overflow`,
  which is `TextOverflow.Clip` — the default. `Average.kt:266` puts a `TextButton` holding a label
  inside `Modifier.size(width = 96.dp, height = 48.dp)`. Those are boxes that cannot grow, and at a
  font scale above 1.0 they are the mechanism behind the sentence the owner read.
- Neither the slot nor the scaffold declares `android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"` or applies
  the IME inset, while `ClassroomTabs.kt:445` shows a text field the keyboard can cover.

Then, on 2026-10-02, he installed a built app on a current phone and reported a second class of
defect, translated from his own words: **"text crawls onto the notification panel, text crawls onto
the camera, and it hides under the bottom buttons"**, written "as if for old phones" — and, in the
same breath, that the app was **"just ordinary buttons and ordinary texts"**. The first sentence is
the edge-to-edge and window-inset defect; the second is the platform-surface defect. Both are
measured in `docs/research/2026-10-02-android-window-insets.md` and
`docs/research/2026-10-02-modern-android-platform-surface.md`, which this rule cites rather than
restates.

`docs/ui-ux-standards.md` is the standard, with the citations. `scripts/core/uiux.mjs` is the sensor,
and it is the only thing that measures this rule.

## Rules

- **Navigation is hosted by Navigation 3, on the back stack Navigation 3 owns.** `NavDisplay` with
  `rememberNavBackStack(…)`, `NavEntry` per destination, and a saved state the host understands. A
  `rememberSaveable` + `mutableStateListOf` + `listSaver` back stack is the defect this rule exists
  for. Check id: **`uiux.navigation`**.
- **A slot with more than one destination reads the window size class.** At least one of
  `currentWindowAdaptiveInfo()` / `WindowSizeClass` / `calculateWindowSizeClass`, or a scene strategy
  (`ListDetailSceneStrategy`, `SupportingPaneSceneStrategy`), or `NavigationSuiteScaffold`, or
  `GridCells.Adaptive`. A list that opens a detail destination must state its two-pane policy.
  Check id: **`uiux.adaptive`**.
- **Text can wrap, and it can grow.** No fixed `Modifier.width` / `height` / `size` on an element that
  holds text, and no `maxLines = 1` without an `overflow`. Text is sized by the Material 3 type
  scale, never shrunk to fit a box; a container grows around its label instead of clipping it.
  Check id: **`uiux.text-layout`**.
- **A multi-screen slot animates.** One of `NavDisplay`'s own transitions, `AnimatedContent`,
  `AnimatedVisibility`, `Crossfade`, `animateContentSize`, `SharedTransitionLayout`, `AnimatedPane`,
  `updateTransition`, `Animatable`, `animateItem` or `animate*AsState`. An instant swap has nothing
  for predictive back to preview. Check id: **`uiux.motion`**.
- **Every interactive element has a name, and every target is at least 48 dp.** An `Icon`/`Image`
  passes a label or an explicit `null`; a clickable element carries a `Text`, a non-null
  `contentDescription` or an `onClickLabel`; nothing declares an extent below `48.dp`.
  Check id: **`uiux.a11y`**.

### Edge-to-edge and insets: the window edge is the design, the chrome is the collision

At `targetSdk 36` edge-to-edge is not a style choice. The window draws behind the status bar, the
navigation bar and the display cutout, Android 16 removes the opt-out entirely, and the platform's
own change list disables the top and bottom offsets so content draws behind the bars "unless insets
are applied" (**MEASURED**, `docs/research/2026-10-02-android-window-insets.md` §1). So a screenshot
taken at `y = 0` proves nothing: **content reaching the window edge is the designed behaviour.** The
defect is one thing only — text and controls drawn *under* the system chrome, which is exactly what
the owner described. The fix is never to retreat from the edge; it is to consume the right insets.

**The subsumption that makes the naive fix wrong.** The types are not interchangeable, and the unions
are MEASURED from the AOSP source (`…insets.md` §2):

```text
safeDrawing  = systemBars ∪ ime ∪ displayCutout
safeGestures = tappableElement ∪ mandatorySystemGestures ∪ systemGestures ∪ waterfall
safeContent  = safeDrawing ∪ safeGestures
```

`systemBars` — and therefore `statusBarsPadding()` and `systemBarsPadding()` — contains **neither the
display cutout nor the IME**. A top punch-hole is usually inside the status bar in portrait, so
`statusBarsPadding()` *looks* correct; rotate to landscape and the cutout is on a vertical edge where
`statusBars` contributes nothing, and the first glyph or toolbar icon sits behind the camera
(**MEASURED**, same doc — that is the owner's camera defect). `safeDrawing` is the union; reach for it.

**A `Scaffold` is not an inset fix by itself.** `ScaffoldDefaults.contentWindowInsets` is
`systemBars ∪ displayCutout`, so it does cover a landscape cutout — but it carries **no IME** — and a
`Scaffold` does not apply its insets to the content slot at all: it hands the content lambda
`PaddingValues` and states that applying them "is your responsibility" (**MEASURED**, `…insets.md`
§4). A `Scaffold` whose `innerPadding` is dropped or never referenced is byte-for-byte as exposed as
no `Scaffold` at all.

**The bottom edge, specifically.** The bottom offset is disabled for both navigation modes, so
anything anchored to the bottom — a FAB, a save button, the last row of a scrollable — lands under the
gesture pill or the 3-button bar unless the inset is applied. A `LazyColumn` with
`Modifier.fillMaxSize()` and no `contentPadding` can scroll its last item to the very bottom, where it
exists but cannot be tapped. The inset belongs **in the scrollable's own `contentPadding`**, not as
`Modifier.padding()` on a parent, so the list may scroll behind the bar and still stop above it
(**MEASURED**, `…insets.md` §5(c)).

**The sane default, in one line.** A screen root outside a `Scaffold` gets
`Modifier.safeDrawingPadding()` (`safeContentPadding()` where system gestures also clash) — the sane
default precisely because it unions the bars, the cutout and the keyboard. Where a `Scaffold` is used,
the content lambda consumes its `innerPadding` (`Modifier.padding(inner).consumeWindowInsets(inner)`),
and a scrollable receives it as `contentPadding` so the last item is reachable. A component's own
inset handling is strengthened, never switched off: `contentWindowInsets = WindowInsets(0, 0, 0, 0)`
on a bar or a `Scaffold` re-introduces the very collisions it was handling.

- **The opt-in, and the keyboard.** Edge-to-edge is opted in explicitly with
  `enableEdgeToEdge()` in the activity, or `WindowCompat.setDecorFitsSystemWindows(window, false)` —
  mandatory at `targetSdk 36`. Whenever the slot shows a text field, the manifest carries
  `android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"` **and** the screen applies an IME inset
  (`imePadding()`, `fitInside(WindowInsetsRulers.Ime…)` or `WindowInsets.ime`); the IME is its own
  inset and `Scaffold`'s default does not carry it. The same check fails a screen that pins chrome
  below a weighted list — a `Surface`/`Column` (or a composable that roots at one) following a
  `weight(…)`/scroll child in a window-filling `Column` — without `navigationBarsPadding()`,
  `windowInsetsPadding(…)`, `safeDrawing`, `WindowInsetsSides.Bottom`, `systemBars` or
  `contentWindowInsets`, because that bar renders under the three-button navigation bar. Check id:
  **`uiux.insets`**.

## The modern platform, and what reaches for it

A correct app that never touches the platform is still "ordinary buttons and ordinary text". This
section is the factory's expectation of the platform surface. Its sensor half is **`uiux.platform`**,
which fails on the two things that are decidable from source alone and **reports the rest as notes**,
because presence is checkable and the *right* use is not.

**Opted in, and checked.** Predictive back is opted in with
`android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"` on `<application>` — one manifest attribute, and its
absence is invisible until a device is used, so `uiux.platform` fails when it is missing. It is also
the platform feature the app's own host must already support: Navigation 3's host-owned back stack is
what the gesture has to preview, which is why `uiux.navigation` and `uiux.motion` are its
precondition. Intercepting back at the root activity disables the back-to-home animation app-wide
(**MEASURED**, `docs/research/2026-10-02-modern-android-platform-surface.md` §2) — so back handling
belongs with the host's back stack, never in `MainActivity`.

**The pinned toolchain is a boundary, not a preference.** Material 3 Expressive and the physics
`MotionScheme` live in `material3 1.5.0-alpha*`; this factory's pinned BOM (`2026.09.00`) ships stable
`material3 1.4.0` and contains neither (**MEASURED**, `…platform-surface.md` §6). Reaching for the
alpha artifact breaks the pinned toolchain every slot shares, so `uiux.platform` **fails** on a build
file that declares an M3 Expressive / `MotionScheme` alpha artifact the pin does not carry. The motion
an app needs is already on the pin: `uiux.motion` lists the animators, `Modifier.animateItem` and the
shared-element APIs.

**Encouraged where the job gives a reason — reported, never gated.** An app is not defective for
lacking one of these, but an app whose job plainly implies one should have it, and `uiux.platform`
reports each as a note rather than a row that turns red:

- the Splash Screen API (`core-splashscreen`, `installSplashScreen()`, a `Theme.SplashScreen` parent)
  — a cold start that looks like the app rather than a white flash;
- share out (`ACTION_SEND`) where the app produces something shareable, and a text-processing target
  (`ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT`) where its job is to transform selected text;
- the Photo Picker (`ActivityResultContracts.PickVisualMedia`) instead of broad media permissions —
  the path Play's own policy prefers (**MEASURED**, `…platform-surface.md` §5);
- haptics (`LocalHapticFeedback`) where a state change has no other confirmation; the semantic type
  is the point and a scanner cannot judge it;
- `Modifier.animateItem` on a list whose order can change, and shared-element transitions where a
  list thumbnail becomes a detail hero;
- `SelectionContainer` where the app's output is text worth copying;
- autofill `Modifier.contentType(...)` on the app's identity fields.

The adaptive vocabulary `uiux.adaptive` measures today is a floor, not a ceiling. Where a slot's job
implies a list and a detail, reach for `ListDetailPaneScaffold` / `currentWindowAdaptiveInfoV2` /
`NavigationSuiteType`; for a foldable, `collectFoldingFeaturesAsState` / `FoldingFeature` — the
research names each as missing from the current set (**ON RECORD**, `…platform-surface.md` §3). None
of this licenses decoration: motion, haptic moments and widget glanceability must stay out of the
gate, because a source scan cannot tell a meaningful transition from a decorative one.

## What is banned

- A hand-rolled back stack behind `NavDisplay`, and a navigation host driven by a list it does not own.
- `Modifier.width(96.dp)` / `Modifier.size(...)` / `Modifier.height(...)` around a label, a chip, a
  pill, a button caption or a table cell.
- `maxLines = 1` with no `overflow` — which renders as `TextOverflow.Clip` and cuts a glyph in half.
- Shrinking a font, or a `fontSize` below the type scale, to make a string fit a box the layout chose.
- A single-column layout for a list and its detail on a tablet or an unfolded phone.
- A hard cut between screens where a transition belongs.
- An icon-only control with no accessible name, and a declared touch target below 48 dp.
- A text field the keyboard can cover, and an app that does not go edge-to-edge at `targetSdk 36`.
- `statusBarsPadding()` or `systemBarsPadding()` as the screen's **sole** inset handling — the
  landscape-cutout collision the owner photographed.
- A `Scaffold` whose `innerPadding` is dropped or never referenced, which is as exposed as no
  `Scaffold`.
- `contentWindowInsets = WindowInsets(0, 0, 0, 0)` (or the same on a bar's `windowInsets`), switching
  off the handling a component already had.
- A full-bleed scrollable whose last row sits under the gesture pill or the 3-button bar because the
  inset was put on a parent `Modifier.padding()` instead of the list's `contentPadding`.
- A manifest that leaves predictive back unopted (`android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback` absent).
- A declared M3 Expressive / `MotionScheme` **alpha** artifact at a pin that does not carry it.
- Weakening `uiux.scaffold` to make a wiring task look finished. The scaffold is the point.

## How it is verified

```bash
node scripts/verify.mjs                       # runs `uiux.scaffold` and `uiux.rule` in the `invariants` group
node scripts/verify.mjs --slot <slug>         # runs the seven `uiux.*` slot checks, in the `ui` group
node --input-type=module -e "import('./scripts/core/uiux.mjs').then(m=>console.log(m.uiuxFindings('apps/<slot>')))"
node --input-type=module -e "import('./scripts/core/uiux.mjs').then(m=>console.log(m.textLayoutFindings('apps/<slot>')))
```

- `scripts/core/uiux.mjs` is the sensor; it reads Kotlin and XML source, **never pixels, never Gradle**.
  It runs no build and spawns no process.
- Findings are `file:line` strings, so a fix is a diff and not a reading. `uiuxFindings()` is the
  whole set; `textLayoutFindings()` is the wrapping/typography subset the owner's review was about.
  `uiux.insets` names a missing opt-in, an unused `innerPadding`, a zeroed bar inset or a
  `statusBars`-only screen; `uiux.platform` names a missing predictive-back opt-in and an alpha
  Expressive dependency, and **reports** the product-conditional surfaces as notes.
- Applicability is derived by `scripts/core/baseline.mjs`, not restated here: a slot created before
  `2026-09-30` (the four that already exist), a pipeline proof with no `economics.json`, and a killed
  slot carry the finding as a **note** inside a PASS; a slot created from today is gated. A finding
  that exists and is not reported would read exactly like a finding that is not there, so the note is
  printed either way.
- `uiuxFixtures()` runs in the `invariants` group and drives every sensor with a synthetic slot tree
  whose verdict is known, so a sensor that has stopped being able to fail is visible in the same
  report as the slots it judges.

## Deliberate under-approximations, named

A static scan that guesses produces false positives, and a noisy gate is read past. These proxies are
**deliberately not taken**, and the reason is part of the rule:

- **A `Text` with no `maxLines` is not a finding.** An unclamped `Text` in a filling or weighted
  container wrapping onto three lines is correct behaviour and is not in scope; the rule fires only on
  `maxLines = 1` *without* an `overflow`.
- **`contentDescription = null` is accepted, not flagged.** Whether an icon is decorative depends on
  the screen around it. Rule `uiux.a11y` fires on an `Icon`/`Image` with **no `contentDescription`
  argument at all**, which is the provable half.
- **A declared extent below 48 dp is checked; an undeclared one is not.** A target that is too small
  because it was never declared cannot be seen statically. `uiux.a11y` catches the declared number,
  which is the case that is provable.
- **A span that already uses `weight(` is skipped by `uiux.text-layout`.** A weighted child is
  flexible by construction, so firing there would be noise. This under-approximates: a fixed extent
  inside a weighted child is missed.
- **`widthIn` / `heightIn` are not fixed extents.** `heightIn(min = 48.dp)` is the *correct* idiom for
  a touch target, and a rule that fired on it would punish the fix for `uiux.a11y`.
- **The scan cannot see a wrapped string.** It names the statically visible *causes* of forced
  wrapping — a box that cannot grow, a clamp that cannot indicate truncation. `docs/ui-ux-standards.md`
  says this in the same words, and the manual half (a `@PreviewFontScales` render, a real device at a
  200 % font scale) stays with the builder and the owner.
- **The scan names the cause of a collision; it cannot see the collision.** `uiux.insets` can point at
  the line that dropped an `innerPadding` or used `statusBarsPadding()` alone, but whether a `Text`
  actually sits under the camera or the notification panel is a runtime fact — inset values depend on
  the device, the orientation and the content. Only a run on the attached phone shows it, which is why
  `.cursor/rules/device-testing.mdc` and the `device-verification.json` record it requires are part of
  this rule rather than beside it: a green `uiux.insets` row means the cause is absent, never that the
  pixels are right. The manual half is named in `…insets.md` §6 — turn on **Simulate a display with a
  cutout**, **rotate to landscape**, and repeat in both **Gesture** and **3-button** navigation.
